1372,Momandme_F,2025-05-29T14:15:37.000Z,"If you're planning a pregnancy, consult your healthcare provider about getting vaccinated against rubella as part of your preconception care. Additionally, practice good hygiene, avoid close contact with anyone showing symptoms, and attend prenatal appointments regularly. 6/7",https://x.com/Momandme_F/status/1928092869916545385,1,1,3,19,NaN
1373,Momandme_F,2025-05-29T14:14:56.000Z,"The Rubella vaccine is highly effective in preventing the infection, but it's essential to get vaccinated before becoming pregnant, as the vaccine contains live virus and is not recommended during pregnancy. 5/?",https://x.com/Momandme_F/status/1928092700336673278,1,1,3,19,NaN
1374,Momandme_F,2025-05-29T14:13:12.000Z,"Symptoms include a low-grade fever, sore throat, and a characteristic rash that starts on the face and spreads to other parts of the body. Contraction of rubella during pregnancy, especially in the first 16 weeks, can lead to Congenital Rubella Syndrome (CRS) in your baby. 3/?",https://x.com/Momandme_F/status/1928092261654389088,1,1,3,30,NaN
1375,Momandme_F,2025-05-29T14:12:23.000Z,"This thread will focus on RUBELLA, one of the prenatal infections that poses a significant risk. Rubella is a highly contagious viral infection that can spread through respiratory droplets when an infected person coughs or sneezes. 2/?",https://x.com/Momandme_F/status/1928092058801123692,1,1,3,25,NaN
